\name{BaseRPairsLayerProcessor}
\alias{BaseRPairsLayerProcessor}
\title{Base R Scatterplot Matrix Processor}
\description{
Reads \code{pairs()} as the grid of scatters it draws: one panel per ordered
pair of columns, the column across plotted against the column down.

\strong{A grid, not a layer.} Every other base R processor answers with one
layer, or several layers in one cell. A scatterplot matrix is \verb{n x n}
\emph{panels}, which is the figure's shape rather than a layer's, so this one
answers with \code{multi_panel = TRUE} and places each layer at its own cell.
That is the shape the same chart already gets elsewhere: \code{sns.pairplot} in
py-maidr, and a plotly \code{splom} since xability/py-maidr#667.

\strong{The grid cannot come from the recording.} Measured, \code{pairs()} sets its
own \code{par(mfrow)} internally and restores it, so nothing lands in the
device's layout calls -- \code{get_layout_calls()} answers zero -- and
\code{detect_panel_configuration()} sees a single panel (#272). The grid is the
reading's own, derived from the number of columns.

\strong{The panels are numbered column-major.} Measured against a real
\code{grid.echo()} export of a three-column matrix, gridGraphics writes nine
\code{graphics-plot-N} groups -- one per cell, diagonal included -- and pairing
them with a \code{panel} that recorded what it was handed gives

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{k    cell     drawn
1    (1,1)    text        the variable's name
2    (2,1)    points      x = column 1, y = column 2
3    (3,1)    points      x = column 1, y = column 3
4    (1,2)    points      x = column 2, y = column 1
5    (2,2)    text
6    (3,2)    points      x = column 2, y = column 3
7    (1,3)    points      x = column 3, y = column 1
8    (2,3)    points      x = column 3, y = column 2
9    (3,3)    text
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

-- that is \code{k = (col - 1) * n + row}, and the panel at \verb{(row, col)} plots
\strong{column \code{col} horizontally against column \code{row} vertically}.

\strong{The diagonal has no layer.} A diagonal cell draws the variable's name
and nothing else, which the orchestrator already has an answer for: a cell
with no layers becomes a valid empty subplot. Giving it a histogram instead
would announce a chart \code{pairs()} does not draw -- its default \code{diag.panel}
draws nothing at all.
}
